<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agent Skills extend LLM agents through reusable
instructions and executable resources, without updating model weights
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>)</cite>. Curated Skills improve
success on tasks requiring specialized expertise
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Acquiring transferable Skills requires
identifying which steps to reuse,
under what conditions, and when to adapt them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent methods distill execution trajectories into Skills and refine
packages through testing and feedback
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. SkillLearnBench’s Teacher
Feedback uses expert advice after failed attempts to guide
revision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Such refinement depends on
which hypotheses the learner tests and
which failures it investigates. Guidance during an ongoing attempt can
redirect these choices before an untested assumption becomes a reusable
rule. We therefore ask: <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how should external expertise guide the
learner while it is still gathering evidence?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Socratic Skill Learning (<span id="S1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SSL</span>)</span>, interleaving
expert teaching with Student practice (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Socratic Skill Learning ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Code,
benchmark, and trajectories:
<a href="https://github.com/NJU-LINK/Socratic-Skill-Learning" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">project repository</a>.</span></span></span>
An Educator observes recent Student work and the live Skill, then explains,
challenges, diagnoses, or remains silent. The Student operates the environment
and authors the package. Inspired by Socratic inquiry, the interaction
examines assumptions through dialogue and practical checks; the Student
can question advice, adapt it, and decide what to retain.
We instantiate SSL with <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fixed-Window SSL</span>, which reviews at regular tool-call
intervals, and <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Issue-Guided SSL</span>, which focuses review on unresolved learning issues.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.17860v1/study_workflow.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of the study.</span>
Panels show (A) task-family construction from six source benchmarks, (B)
acquisition-protocol comparisons, external methods, and model pairs, and
(C) the path from teaching demand to issue-guided review with utility and cost.
Panel C summarizes Qwen experiments on the 22-family main cohort.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Existing benchmarks measure Skill utility and reuse
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Studying guided acquisition
also requires specifying what the learner should investigate beyond the
practice task. We construct
<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SkillTransferBench</span>, a benchmark of 35 <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">task families</span> and
408 executable cases across six domains. Each family pairs an
anchor task with a public specification of invariants and permitted variation.
This specification guides exploration; fresh solvers test the acquired
package on held-out family members.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We organize the study around four questions (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):
<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> How well does SSL transfer relative to existing induction methods?
<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> How should external expertise guide Skill acquisition?
<span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> How does guidance enter Skill development as learning progresses?
<span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4:</span> How should review be organized to sustain transfer with less
expert supervision?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">On shared families across three model pairs, both interleaved protocols
outperform direct expert authoring and post-hoc teaching. Process analysis
links lessons to Student development; a case study shows the Student
testing an Educator-proposed procedure and refining it for later reuse.
<span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Issue-Guided SSL</span> reaches 0.805 utility with
61.7% fewer Educator tokens on the main cohort.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our contributions are threefold:</span></p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SkillTransferBench</span>, containing 35
task families and 408 executable cases across six domains,
with public family specifications that guide acquisition and held-out
evaluation that tests frozen Skills in fresh solver sessions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Socratic Skill Learning (<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SSL</span>), in which the
Student owns exploration, verification, and Skill authoring while an
Educator guides practice. Issue-Guided SSL uses persistent learning issues
to select and focus review around unresolved questions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Skill transfer across three model pairs and complement
these comparisons with process analysis and case studies, linking guidance
to Student development and showing how selecting and focusing review
sustains transfer with less expert supervision.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Constructing Skills from execution evidence.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Skills can retain experience as guidance or executable code
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, consolidate trajectory lessons
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, or evolve through execution and verification
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. SKILL-KD’s consolidation agent
derives patches from Teacher–Student trajectory contrasts and validates
them through Student reruns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feedback during Skill development.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-Refine and Reflexion improve outputs or attempts through feedback
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For agent diagnosis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>,
CodeTracer reconstructs state transitions to localize failures
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. SkillLearnBench uses a
reference Skill to guide revision after failure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2026</a>)</cite>;
AgentBrew validates teacher-authored corrections through student retries
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang and Huang, 2026</a>)</cite>. HASP includes teacher review of student-proposed
skill programs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, while SkillHone preserves revision
evidence and decisions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Hu, 2026</a>)</cite>. SSL places Educator guidance
within Student investigation and verification, leaving the Student to
author the Skill. Unresolved learning questions select and focus review.
This design connects Socratic dialogue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> with learner
construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chi et al., 2001</a>)</cite> and feedback on the learning process
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hattie and Timperley, 2007</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scope and transfer in Skill evaluation.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SkillsBench measures Skill utility <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>; SkillLearnBench
and EconSkills evaluate reuse across parameterized instances
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quan and Liu, 2026</a>)</cite>. Trace2Skill tests held-out
transfer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, while ContinualSkillBench uses ordered
task streams <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SkillTransferBench</span>
defines acquisition scope through a public family specification. Across
six domains, evaluation in fresh sessions connects guided acquisition to
held-out transfer and supervision cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>SkillTransferBench</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Task families and Skill transfer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Skill acquisition develops a reusable procedure for a task family <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Acquisition uses an executable anchor <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{f,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{f,0}</annotation></semantics></math> for practice and a public
specification <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> defining invariant goals and permitted variation
(e.g., WAL encoding, integrity, and recovery depth). The specification directs
investigation beyond the anchor; held-out family members test transfer
across these variations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The acquired Skill is a package <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}=(M,\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=(M,\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, containing
instructions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\texttt{SKILL.md}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">SKILL.md</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\texttt{SKILL.md}</annotation></semantics></math> and optional scripts, references, and
assets <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. We evaluate the frozen package with the target solver <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>
in a new session for each case (a fresh solver). Transfer utility is</p>
