<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Socratic Skill Learning</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Socratic Skill Learning (SSL), in which an Educator guides
ongoing investigation while a Student tests the guidance and authors a
transferable Skill. We develop two instantiations: <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fixed-Window SSL</span>
periodically reviews Student progress, while <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Issue-Guided SSL</span> maintains
learning issues to select and focus subsequent reviews. We first describe
their shared learning process (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Socratic Skill Learning ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>A), then present
each protocol (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Socratic Skill Learning ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>B).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.17860v1/insight.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/351;" width="476" height="351"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Socratic Skill Learning.</span>
<span id="S4.F3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span> The Student tests guidance and authors the Skill; the Educator reviews
recent work and the live package read-only. Only the Skill transfers to fresh solvers.
<span id="S4.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span> <span id="S4.F3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fixed-Window SSL</span> reviews every <math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> tool calls; <span id="S4.F3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Issue-Guided SSL</span> reviews unresolved issues when they open, change, or reopen, using focused context.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Socratic dialogue and Student practice</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both protocols use the target solver’s model as the Student, the sole
environment operator and author of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Guided by the family
specification, it explores the anchor, tests candidate procedures, and
decides what evidence to gather and what knowledge to retain
(Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Task families and Skill transfer ‣ 3 SkillTransferBench ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Educator review and Student response.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Educator has read-only access to recent Student work and the live Skill.
It can explain, challenge, diagnose, propose checks, or remain silent.
The Student can question, test, adapt, or reject advice, responding through
dialogue, environment experiments, and package revisions. This interaction
examines the assumptions behind a procedure and the evidence needed to
retain it, with the Student deciding how the evidence changes the Skill.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rounds and transfer.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fresh solver tests each round’s package on the reset anchor, checking
whether it carries the procedure beyond the author’s session. The resulting
trajectory and verifier feedback guide the next round. Student and Educator
sessions persist; evaluation sessions are fresh. Only the final package
transfers to held-out family members.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Fixed-Window SSL</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fixed-Window SSL organizes acquisition as repeated periods of Student
practice and Educator review. After every <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> completed Student tool calls,
the Educator examines recent reasoning and tool results alongside the live
Skill, informed by the family specification, prior lessons, and anchor
feedback. This recurring review can challenge an assumption or suggest a
missing check while an investigation is still open, shaping the evidence
gathered before a procedure is retained.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Student can clarify or contest a lesson, then resumes exploration and
Skill revision. The resulting
work informs the next review. Silence lets practice continue directly.
The schedule thus fixes when review occurs, while guidance responds to
the Student’s progress. The Student determines when to finish the round;
a final review covers any work since the previous review, allowing Student
action before the snapshot (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Acquisition loop and review events ‣ Appendix C Protocols, Implementation, and Reproduction ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Issue-Guided SSL</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Teaching demand changes as a Skill develops: practice can settle a
question, while new evidence can call for further guidance. Issue-Guided
SSL tracks unresolved questions across revisions to select when review
is needed and focus it on relevant evidence.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Persistent learning issues.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An Educator lesson supplies a learning focus and an acceptance criterion:
what to investigate and what observable evidence would resolve it. A learning
issue retains these together with evidence and package revisions for
subsequent review. Issues form a persistent
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Learning-Issue Tree</span>: a concern arising within an investigation becomes a child;
closing it returns attention to the nearest unresolved ancestor. Resolved
branches preserve what has already been addressed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Revisions and resolution.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Student groups file edits into a coherent revision, declaring its
summary, evidence, and any issue addressed. An addressed issue can close
with a changed package when the Student verifies the revision, documents
the procedure’s applicability with evidence, or rejects the guidance on
evidential grounds. Verified closure requires a successful post-lesson
check. Investigation can therefore settle a question without another
Educator call.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Selective review and reopening.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An initial review occurs at the first revision or after a warm-up window
of tool calls. Thereafter, an unresolved issue must open, change, or reopen
before further review. The Educator receives the active issue and its
ancestors, the latest revision, and recent local work, with read access to
the complete package. A lesson returns the Student to investigation;
silence closes the current review episode. Contradictory
fresh-solver anchor feedback can reopen the associated issue in the next
round while preserving resolved siblings.
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Acquisition loop and review events ‣ Appendix C Protocols, Implementation, and Reproduction ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a> gives event rules and boundary handling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Can SSL Develop Transferable Agent Skills?</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess how teaching supports Skill acquisition, we investigate four
research questions (RQs): <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span> evaluates transfer to held-out family
members; <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span> compares how expert guidance is organized across model
pairs; <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span> traces teaching, Student actions, and package revisions
during acquisition; and <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4</span> examines whether issue-guided review
preserves transfer while reducing expert supervision. Evaluations follow
the shared protocol in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 SkillTransferBench ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>RQ1: Transfer to held-out family members</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Do Skills acquired through interleaved teaching transfer to unseen
members of the same task families?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 RQ1: Transfer to held-out family members ‣ 5 Can SSL Develop Transferable Agent Skills? ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares overall and domain-level transfer on
the Qwen 22-family, 155-case main cohort.
Qwen3.7 Plus serves as Student and fresh solver; Qwen3.7 Max supplies
external expertise as Educator or direct package author.
We compare the two SSL schedules with No Skill, direct Expert
Authoring, Post-hoc Teaching, and adapted Trace2Skill and SKILL-KD.
SSL, Expert Authoring, and Post-hoc Teaching use 3 acquisition
rounds, with <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=10</annotation></semantics></math> for Fixed-Window SSL. Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Protocols, Implementation, and Reproduction ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>
details model settings, baseline adaptations, and session boundaries;
Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Supplementary Transfer Results ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> provides per-family results,
case-level gains and regressions, and uncertainty estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The two SSL schedules rank first and second overall and in each of the
six domains. Fixed-Window SSL exceeds adapted Trace2Skill by
3.99 percentage points (95% paired family-bootstrap CI:
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.45,6.49]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1.45</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6.49</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.45,6.49]</annotation></semantics></math>) and adapted SKILL-KD by
4.99 points. These gains measure package reuse beyond
the anchor and acquisition session.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The utility improvement extends to complete task solutions. Family-macro
full-pass rate, requiring every private check to pass, rises from
<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32.50</annotation></semantics></math>% without a Skill
to <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>49.49</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.49</annotation></semantics></math>% with
Fixed-Window SSL and
<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50.82</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.82</annotation></semantics></math>% with
Issue-Guided SSL.</p>
</div>
