<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>RQ2: Organizing expert guidance during Skill acquisition</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How should external expertise guide Skill acquisition?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 RQ2: Organizing expert guidance during Skill acquisition ‣ 5 Can SSL Develop Transferable Agent Skills? ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a) compares the four authoring protocols on the shared
10 families across the Qwen, DeepSeek, and Kimi model pairs,
complemented by the Qwen main cohort. Within each pair, the solver and tasks are fixed; protocols
differ in who investigates and writes the package, when guidance is available,
and how the agents interact (Appendices <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Protocols, Implementation, and Reproduction ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>
and <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Supplementary Transfer Results ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Guidance during practice consistently produces stronger transfer than
reviewing the Student’s completed authoring trajectory. Fixed-Window SSL
and Post-hoc Teaching both preserve the Student as environment operator
and package author, but Fixed-Window SSL outperforms Post-hoc Teaching on all three
model pairs. On the main cohort, this advantage is 5.31
percentage points in utility with 21% fewer
acquisition tokens.
Interleaving lets the Student test advice while an investigation is still
open and use the resulting evidence in subsequent exploration and writing.
Post-hoc review arrives after those earlier choices have shaped the package.
The results favor feedback that can redirect evidence gathering throughout
practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The ordering also distinguishes guided practice from simply retaining a
Student author. On the shared families, Post-hoc Teaching trails Expert
Authoring in every model pair, whereas both interleaved protocols surpass
Expert Authoring. This ordering favors coupling Student authorship with
expert guidance during investigation. RQ3 examines how this interaction
appears in Student actions and package revisions.</p>
</div>
