<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S5.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Teaching demand, Student development, and review cost.</span>
Qwen Fixed-Window SSL (<math id="S5.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=10</annotation></semantics></math>), 22 families and 66 acquisition runs.
Panels show (a) anchor scores and teaching rates at regular reviews,
(b) teaching–silence differences in writing on the next tool call and
Skill changes over ten calls, standardized by round with 95% family-bootstrap
intervals, and (c) tokens per review before follow-up, with silent shares.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Teaching demand falls while Skill development continues. As anchor utility
improves, the fraction of regular reviews delivering a lesson falls from
73.0% in round one to 16.1% in round
three, and advice shifts from reasoning toward diagnosis. Under the
three-round protocol, packages still change in 98
of 99 rounds following a perfect anchor score,
including rounds with no new lesson.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Compared with silence, lessons are followed by a
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>23.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.9</annotation></semantics></math>-point higher
probability of file writing or editing on the next call and a
<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>38.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38.6</annotation></semantics></math>-point higher
probability of a Skill change over the next ten calls, after adjustment for
round (Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 RQ3: How teaching enters Skill development ‣ 5 Can SSL Develop Transferable Agent Skills? ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>b).</p>
</div>
