<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Benchmark Assets and Evaluation Cohorts</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Construction routes</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experts in coding and agent benchmarking collaborate with agents to construct
the task families. We first convert source tasks to a common format,
reconciling paths, formats, ordering, precision, and boundary conditions
across the task descriptions, fixtures, and scorers. For example, adapting
the WAL anchor preserves the source database fixtures while revising task
metadata and strengthening checks of the recovered output.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In extraction and adaptation, compatible source cases are grouped into
families, and statements and tests are revised to align their requirements.
In seed expansion, a case from the adapted pool seeds a specification of the
shared invariant and permitted variation; experts and agents then develop
additional cases within this scope. Each task includes a description,
public fixtures, a resettable environment, a reference solution, and a
private verifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For example, WAL-recovery cases vary encoding, integrity, and recovery depth
while preserving the reconstruction goal. Acquisition uses a fixed
<span id="A2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">v01</span> anchor in each family.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Catalog and variation axes</h3>

