<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Protocols, Implementation, and Reproduction</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Runtime, sessions, and package boundaries</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Runtime and models.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We abbreviate the three model pairs as Qwen, DeepSeek, and Kimi. In each
pair, the Student model also serves as the fresh solver, while the Educator
model supplies external expertise or writes the package under Expert
Authoring.
The harness uses Pi coding agent 0.81.1<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://pi.dev" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://pi.dev</a></span></span></span>,
TypeScript 5.9.3, and Docker task environments. Main Qwen runs use
<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qwen3.7-plus</span> as Student and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qwen3.7-max</span> as Educator,
with a 1,000,000-token
context window, a 65,536-token maximum completion, and thinking enabled.
Sampling uses the provider defaults.
DeepSeek uses <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">deepseek-v4-flash-0731</span> as Student and
<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">deepseek-v4-pro-0813</span> as Educator,
with the same context window, 393,216 maximum completion tokens,
thinking enabled, and reasoning effort <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max</span>.
Kimi uses <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kimi-k2.5</span> for the Student and fresh solvers
(262,144-token context, 16,384 maximum completion, thinking enabled),
and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kimi-k3</span> for the external author or Educator
(1,048,576-token context, 131,072 maximum completion, reasoning effort
<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max</span>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Session isolation and access.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within an original authoring-protocol run, the package author (the external
model under Expert Authoring; the Student otherwise) and, where applicable,
the Educator maintain separate continuous sessions across acquisition rounds,
with automatic context compaction. Sessions never continue across families,
replicates, or protocol cells. Every anchor and held-out evaluation uses a
fresh target-model solver. Adapted SKILL-KD instead uses fresh executions
with persistent rule-edit history (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3.SSS1" title="C.3.1 Adapted SKILL-KD ‣ C.3 Acquisition comparators ‣ Appendix C Protocols, Implementation, and Reproduction ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The author operates in a Docker workspace containing the public task, anchor
inputs, and family specification, and is the only agent that writes the live
Skill. Educator tools list, read, and search the full package but cannot execute
environment actions or write files. Solvers load only the frozen target package
through the native Skill catalog, with ambient Skills disabled and a clean case
workspace. The package contains <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SKILL.md</span> and optional
<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">scripts/</span>, <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">references/</span>, and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assets/</span> directories.
Held-out materials remain inaccessible during acquisition, and private checks
are applied only after the solver submits its solution. Snapshots retain every
package file; resource
reads and executable invocations are logged.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Acquisition loop and review events</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Review context and interaction.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fixed-Window SSL</span> reviews after every <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> completed Student tool calls, with
<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=10</annotation></semantics></math> in the main condition and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5,20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5,20</annotation></semantics></math> in the schedule comparison.
<span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fixed-Window SSL</span> provides the Educator with the family definition, recent
reasoning and tool calls, prior lessons, feedback from anchor tests by fresh
solvers, and the complete live package. An opening lesson may explain, propose
a test, diagnose, or
challenge an assumption; the Student may clarify, adapt, or contest it,
followed by at most one Educator reply. Subsequent task actions and package
edits remain Student decisions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Round completion.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The author ends its trajectory when it has finished the current exploration
and package revision. The harness completes the scheduled terminal review,
if any, and the Student’s resulting work, then validates and snapshots the
package for a fresh anchor test. Each round permits at most one terminal
review; resulting Student work is retained without another terminal review.
The tool-call window controls review frequency, while the author determines
trajectory length. All 3 rounds run, with anchor feedback entering
the next round.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.fig1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.fig1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The loop below preserves Student package ownership under both schedules.</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><pre id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.fig1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_figure_panel ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">
create Student; create Educator; initialize package K
for round in 1..3:
  give Student public spec, clean anchor, previous feedback
  while the current authoring trajectory is active:
    let Student reason, invoke tools, and edit K
    Fixed-Window SSL: review after g completed tool calls
      or at an unreviewed end-of-round tail
    Issue-Guided SSL: on bootstrap, revision commit, or contradiction:
      resolve addressed active issue if closure conditions hold
      otherwise update, open, or reopen its persistent issue
      request review only for an unresolved transition
    if review requested:
      Educator observes local evidence and complete package
      if lesson: Student responds
        fixed-window permits one Educator follow-up
      else: retain Issue-Guided SSL issue with no-lesson closure reason
      Student resumes its own work
  freeze K; evaluate with fresh target model on clean anchor
  retain trajectory and verifier feedback for the next round
evaluate final K with a fresh target model for each held-out case
</pre></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Revision declarations and issue state.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During practice, the Student calls <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">commit_skill_revision</span> after a
coherent knowledge change to the complete package. The declaration includes
a summary and evidence, plus an issue ID and disposition when it addresses
an existing lesson. Ordinary file edits accumulate until the Student commits
such a revision. The Educator supplies a learning focus and an observable
acceptance criterion when delivering a lesson.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each persistent issue stores a focus, acceptance criterion, linked revisions,
evidence, and disposition. A child records a concern within ongoing work;
closure returns attention to the nearest unresolved ancestor. In Issue-Guided
SSL, the first-round bootstrap occurs at the first revision or after ten tool
calls, whichever comes first. At the round boundary, an uncommitted package change is processed as
an implicit revision; the runtime also handles any pending bootstrap or anchor
contradiction before the final snapshot. The default Educator view includes up to three active-path nodes, two
recently closed issues, the latest revision, and recent local tool calls;
read access to the complete package remains available. After bootstrap, another
unresolved transition precedes further teaching, so triggers, context, and
follow-up policy change together.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Student-declared closure requires an addressed active issue and a changed
package. A verified disposition additionally requires a successful post-lesson
verification event; bounded and rejected dispositions record stated evidence.
A no-lesson closure records Educator silence rather than verified correctness.
Contradictory feedback from an anchor test by a fresh solver targets the issue
associated with that package and may reopen it while preserving resolved siblings.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Acquisition comparators</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Expert Authoring assigns exploration and package writing to the external model,
including feedback from multi-round anchor tests by fresh solvers. Post-hoc
Teaching keeps the Student as author and offers one review after the authoring
trajectory in each round. A lesson is followed by Student checks and revisions;
this protocol has no immediate Educator follow-up. Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Cost accounting. ‣ C.3 Acquisition comparators ‣ Appendix C Protocols, Implementation, and Reproduction ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>
summarizes the interaction structure. No Skill
skips acquisition and solves each case in a fresh session. Our adaptation of the
released Trace2Skill retains parallel patch proposal and consolidation over
independent anchor trajectories generated from a generic seed package, replacing the
spreadsheet-specific front end with a task-family adapter. The main authoring
comparison contains 198 acquisition runs; Issue-Guided SSL adds 66.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cost accounting.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Acquisition tokens include cached input and exclude held-out solving.
Qwen totals cover the author, Educator, and fresh-solver anchor evaluations;
DeepSeek and Kimi totals cover the persistent author and Educator sessions.
Teaching efficiency divides transfer gain by Educator tokens.</p>
</div>
