</table>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning is the most common
opening intent; diagnosis and verification are more frequent in follow-ups.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Review decisions and subsequent Student behavior</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We relate teaching and silence to the Student’s next action and subsequent
Skill revision. The analysis covers 66 <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fixed-Window SSL</span> runs from
the evaluation manifest, using the latest complete attempt for each family
and repeat. They contain 630 reviews, 254
opening lessons, and 91 follow-ups. Regular ten-tool-call
reviews include 226 teaching and 206
silent decisions; trajectory-end reviews include
28 and 170, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Observation windows.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each window begins when the Student receives a lesson or a silent review
completes, and ends at the next review or round boundary. We use event order
and tool-call IDs to identify actions begun within the window. Initial
prompts delimit rounds; recovery messages remain within the current round.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across 432 regular windows, we classify the first
subsequent tool call as environment, write/edit, read/search, or absent.
Trajectory-end reviews are analyzed separately:
24/28 lessons precede
a tool call, while all 170 silent reviews end the round.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Skill revision over ten tool calls.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The revision analysis uses 193 windows containing
exactly ten calls begun after review, with no continuation of an earlier call.
We compare the Skill versions at the start and end of each window.
Both versions are observable in 112 windows; the other
81 lack a comparable pair. Among these
112 windows, the package changes in
67/69 teaching windows and
27/43 silent windows.
A sensitivity analysis relaxes the ten-call restriction and includes
316 windows with comparable versions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimation.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These observational comparisons measure associations with teaching:
review decisions respond to the Student’s ongoing work. We standardize
contrasts by acquisition round, using rounds with both comparison groups
and weighting them by the eligible sample’s round distribution.
Table <a href="#A5.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ Writing and revision after teaching. ‣ E.2 Review decisions and subsequent Student behavior ‣ Appendix E Teaching Process Analysis ‣ Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> reports the number of supported observations
with known outcomes (<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>). Confidence intervals resample the
22 whole families, keeping each family’s runs and reviews
together and retaining the round weights.
Pre-review comparisons use recorded tool composition, error flags, shell
exit status, and package state. Tool-error and no-error groups contain
111 and 321 regular windows;
the observable/unobservable package comparison is supported in round one.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Writing and revision after teaching.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following teaching, the Student’s next tool call is
<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>23.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.9</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points
more likely to write or edit a file than following silence
(95% CI: <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[13.0,36.1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>13.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>36.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[13.0,36.1]</annotation></semantics></math>). Over the next ten tool calls, the Skill package
is <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>38.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38.6</annotation></semantics></math> points more
likely to change (<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[10.7,57.8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>10.7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>57.8</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[10.7,57.8]</annotation></semantics></math>), after standardizing by round.
A within-run check gives a mean package-change difference of
34.3 points over 18 runs
containing both decision types.</p>
</div>
